id: pm-0mhspz
title: Custom statuses registered without a lifecycle role are silently orphaned from every work-selection surface
description: "pm schema add-status accepts a status with no --role and registers it with an empty roles list, with no warning. Items in that status then exist but are invisible to the entire agent work-selection surface, and no diagnostic reports the condition. Reproduced on a clean sandbox workspace against dist build 2026.7.25.\n\nRepro: pm schema add-status needs_review (exit 0, no warning; schema/statuses.json records {\"id\":\"needs_review\"} with no roles key, unlike every built-in which carries roles such as active/terminal/default_open). pm create --status needs_review succeeds. Then: pm next -> recommended null, ready 0, candidates 0, blocked 0. pm context -> active_items 0, open 0. pm list-open -> 0 rows. pm validate -> ok true with no role-related warning. pm health -> ok true, all 10 checks ok. The item IS visible to pm stats by_status and to pm list --status needs_review, so it is not terminal either: it is lifecycle-orphaned.\n\nControl: registering the same status with --role active makes the identical item recommended by pm next (ready 1, candidates 1) and active in pm context (active_items 1). The single missing role flag is the whole difference between fully tracked work and work no agent will ever be offered.\n\nSecond half of the same class: pm context summary.open and summary.in_progress are computed by matching the literal built-in status ids open and in_progress (src/cli/commands/context.ts around lines 2645-2654 call normalizeStatusInput with hardcoded \"open\"/\"in_progress\"), while summary.active_items is role-derived. In the --role active control above the result is active_items 1 with open 0 and in_progress 0, so the summary arithmetic silently stops adding up in any workspace that uses a custom active status. Agents read that summary to decide whether work exists.\n\nThis directly contradicts epic pm-8jdc acceptance ('any project archetype can model its domain end-to-end with custom statuses without code changes') and is exactly the class the pm-pww8ax dogfood ratchet exists to catch: custom statuses are at zero adoption in this workspace, so the orphaning has never been exercised."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[4]: agent-ux,"area:cli","area:schema",customization
created_at: "2026-07-25T21:12:41.727Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-25T21:40:48.466Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: "pm schema add-status either requires a lifecycle role or defaults to a documented role and reports which role was applied; registering a role-less status is impossible or warns loudly at registration time. pm validate and pm health each gain a check that reports every registered status carrying no lifecycle role, with the affected item ids. pm context summary.open and summary.in_progress are derived from lifecycle roles rather than the literal built-in status ids, so active_items reconciles with its component buckets in a workspace using custom active statuses. A regression test registers a custom status per lifecycle role and asserts next/context/list-open/list-blocked classify its items consistently with the equivalent built-in status."
parent: pm-8jdc
risk: medium
confidence: high
dependencies[2]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind}:
  pm-8jdc,implements,"2026-07-25T21:12:41.727Z",null,null
  pm-pww8ax,related,"2026-07-25T21:12:41.727Z",null,null
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T21:40:48.466Z","harness:claude-code","Reproduction extended 2026-07-25: the second half of this class ships inside a first-party profile, so it is reachable without anyone mis-using pm schema add-status.\n\npm profile apply research registers the custom status 'analyzing' correctly — schema/statuses.json records roles [\"active\"], pm schema show-status analyzing reports source custom, roles active, order 12, and pm profile lint research reports ok with 0 warnings. Everything the workspace is told about the status is right.\n\nAn item moved into it then behaves inconsistently across the read surface:\n  pm next            recommends it, reasons \"open and ready to start\", ready 1   (role-derived, correct)\n  pm context         active_items 1, open 0, in_progress 0                        (mixed, the arithmetic stops adding up)\n  pm list-open       count 0                                                      (literal status id, wrong)\n  pm validate        ok true, pm health ok true                                   (silent)\n\nSo list-open belongs to the literal-id half alongside context summary.open / summary.in_progress, and this item's acceptance criterion already names it in the regression-test clause. Worth keeping because it changes the severity argument: the fix is not only about guarding a mis-used flag, it is about the three shipped profiles (agile, ops, research) each adding an active-role status whose items are already invisible to list-open today. Any workspace that applies a built-in profile — the intended path for customising pm to a domain — lands in the inconsistent state without doing anything unusual."
body: ""
